                                 Rules

This game was designed by Sid Sackson, and is described in his 1969
book, "A Gamut of Games".

A bowling score pad consists of 10 frames.  In each frame, a player can
roll two balls in an attempt to knock down the 10 pins.  If all 10 are
knocked down on the first ball (a strike = X), the second ball is not
used.

In each frame, the player scores as many pins as he knocks down.  If a
strike is scored, the number of pins downed with the next 2 balls is
added to the 10 for the strike.

If the player knocks down the remaining pins with the second ball (a
spare = /), the number of pins downed with the next ball is added to
the 10 for the spare.

Additional balls are allowed in the final frame if a spare or strike is
scored.  Up to 3 balls might be played.

Maximum score 300 = 12 strikes

Changes for bowling solitaire:

1 card from the top of a ball pile can be used for removing 1-3 cards
from the pin layout.  The pin cards must be adjacent and, when added
together, their last digit must be the same as the ball pile card.

The card from the ball pile and the card(s) from the layout are
removed.  All following plays must remove pin cards adjacent to removed
pins.

The ball is over when no more cards from the ball pile can be used, or
if play of such a card would make later plays impossible due to lack of
necessary cards already played.

If a strike is not scored, the top card from each of the ball piles is
removed, and play continues again.  If all the remaining pins are
removed with the second ball, a spare is scored.

The first card of a frame cannot be used to remove any card from the
back row, or to remove the middle pin by itself.


                                Playing

The keys used to control Bowling (on a US keyboard - see below for
modifying for other keyboards) are as follows:

    Pin Layout Keys:                        Ball Pile Keys:

            1   2   3   4                       9 0 +
              Q   W   E
                A   S
                  Z



<Enter> Ball over

<Esc> Clear all selected pins

Basic Play Sequence:

Use the Pin keys to select up to three adjacent pins, then use a ball
pile key to remove the selected pins.

Continue until no more can be removed.  If pins remain, press <Enter>.
Strikes and spares are automatically processed.

For advanced play (and according to the recommended play in the
book), use the play table above the score table.  This indicates which
cards have been taken out of play.  The deck is made up of 20 cards -
two sets of 1-10.  Thus when only one of the cards has been taken out
of play, a '/' appears under the number.  When both cards are out of
play, a 'X' appears under the rank.


                               Compiling

Bowling was written completely with PLIB, HWIF, and WLIB functions,
and, as such, is not portable (even to other SIBO machines, unless you
want to rewrite all the HWIF sections) in any way.  The source to the
game and the utilities library necessary to compile are freely
available from the author.

Attention non-English speakers:

It is possible to translate and compile new language-dependent resource
files (without having to recompile the whole program with the C
compiler) and BOWLING will look for them in the \APP\BOWL\ directory on
any drive.  Using the RCOMP resource compiler (available from Psion),
RCOMP XXXXXX.RC will generate a suitable XXXXXX.RSC resource file.

As a preliminary move, I have translated just the keys for German and
French keyboards in the BOWL03.RSC and BOWL02.RSC resources.  The
resource can be altered by changing and recompiling the BOWLXX.RC
files - available from the author.

Notes on translation:

When translating the file, only strings in quotes should be altered.
Care should be taken to keep all RESOURCEs in the same order, and in
the case of the help topics, care should be taken with the line
lengths.  The file should be named BOWLXX.RSC where XX is the
two-letter language code of your machine.

01  English
02  French
03  German
04  Spanish
05  Italian
06  Swedish
07  Danish
08  Norwegian
09  Finnish
10  USA     (This is embedded in the application already)
11  Swiss French
12  Swiss German
13  Portuguese
14  Turkish
15  Icelandic
16  Russian
17  Hungarian
18  Dutch
19  Belgian Flemish
20  Australian
21  New Zealand
22  Austrian
23  Belgian French

If a suitably named file exists, it will always be used.  If not, the
embedded resource (American English) will be used.  Thus it is possible
to create a resource file (in any language) and name it suitably for
your machine and put it in a \APP\BOWL\ directory.  e.g.  Your machine
is German but you speak French.  Just compile a French resource and
rename it with the German code:  BOWL03.RSC instead of BOWL02.RSC.
Thus it is always possible to test any external resource file by
renaming it to your machine's language code - overriding even use of
the internal file.  The English resource script is named BOWLING.RC,
and is freely available.  Other .RC files should be named
appropriately:  e.g.  BOWLXX.RC.  These will be added to later
distributions if mailed to the author.


